## Scenario 1: Customer Support Resolution Agent

**1.1 (Domain 1)** — Which control flow correctly determines when to keep executing tools and when to present the final response?
A. Loop until text contains a completion phrase
B. Inspect `stop_reason`: "tool_use" → execute and loop; "end_turn" → present final response
C. Run exactly five iterations
D. Stop as soon as any response contains text content

**1.2 (Domain 1)** — How does the model actually receive tool result data?
A. MCP server pushes result directly into model memory
B. Model re-reads tool description, now including result
C. Nothing required — API executes tools automatically
D. Your code appends a tool result block (referencing the tool call's ID) to conversation history and resends to Claude

**1.3 (Domain 1)** — Refund cap of $500 is occasionally bypassed despite prompt instructions. Correct enforcement design?
A. Hook intercepting outgoing `process_refund` calls, blocking amounts >$500, redirecting to escalation
B. Move limit to top of system prompt, repeat every turn
C. Lower temperature
D. Add limit to tool description

**1.4 (Domain 1)** — Customer message contains three concerns (refund, address change, loyalty credit). How to handle?
A. Resolve only the first, wait for others to be re-raised
B. Escalate immediately — multi-issue always exceeds capability
C. Decompose into three items, investigate each (parallel where possible) using shared context, synthesize one unified resolution
D. Ask customer to resend each separately

**1.5 (Domain 1)** — Escalation handoff to humans without transcript access. What should it include?
A. Only customer name
B. Structured handoff summary: customer ID, root cause analysis, amounts/order numbers, recommended action
C. A sentiment score
D. Raw JSON of every tool result

**1.6 (Domain 2)** — `process_refund` failure returns generic "Operation failed," causing wrong retry behavior. Fix?
A. Instruct agent to retry exactly twice
B. Remove failure responses entirely
C. Have agent apologize on any error
D. Return structured error metadata: `errorCategory` (transient/validation/business/permission), `isRetryable` boolean, human-readable description

**1.7 (Domain 2)** — Refund violates returns window policy; tool rejects it. Which error response is correct?
A. `errorCategory: "business"`, `retriable: false`, customer-friendly explanation
B. `errorCategory: "transient"` so agent retries later
C. Empty result marked success
D. A stack trace

**1.8 (Domain 2, select TWO)** — Agent confuses `get_customer` and `lookup_order`. Which TWO description changes fix misrouting?
A. Make descriptions shorter
B. Document input formats and add example queries **✓**
C. Prefix with "IMPORTANT:"
D. Add boundary language differentiating tool use cases **✓**
E. Rely on tool names alone

**1.9 (Domain 5)** — Customer's first message: "I want to speak to a human. Do not try to fix this yourself." Issue looks routine. What should agent do?
A. Attempt resolution first, escalate only if it fails
B. Run sentiment analysis, escalate only if frustration exceeds threshold
C. Escalate immediately — explicit customer request for a human is honored without first attempting investigation
D. Ask three clarifying questions first

**1.10 (Domain 5, select TWO)** — Long sessions misquote amounts/order numbers due to lossy summarization; tool results dump 40+ fields into context. Which TWO changes fix this?
A. Extract transactional facts into a persistent case-facts block outside summarized history **✓**
B. Increase `max_tokens`
C. Summarize even more aggressively
D. Trim each tool result to only fields relevant to the current issue before it enters context **✓**
E. Ask customer to repeat key numbers

