# Answer Key & Short Rationales

### Scenario 1
| # | Ans | Why |
|---|-----|-----|
| 1.1 | B | `stop_reason` is the designed termination signal — "tool_use" vs "end_turn," not text-pattern guessing or arbitrary caps. |
| 1.2 | D | No auto-memory; your code appends a tool_result block and resends full history on the next call. |
| 1.3 | A | Deterministic guarantees for financial rules need code-level interception (hooks), not probabilistic prompt compliance. |
| 1.4 | C | Multi-issue decomposition within shared context protects first-contact resolution; don't drop or bounce stated concerns. |
| 1.5 | B | Structured handoff transfers case state so a human without transcript access doesn't redo the investigation. |
| 1.6 | D | Structured error metadata (category/retryable/description) lets the agent choose the correct recovery path. |
| 1.7 | A | Policy violations are non-retryable business errors; retriable:false + explanation avoids futile retries. |
| 1.8 | B, D | Input formats + examples plus explicit boundary language are what actually disambiguate similar tools. |
| 1.9 | C | Explicit stated instructions outrank inferred signals (e.g., sentiment) — honor them immediately. |
| 1.10 | A, D | Persist precise facts outside lossy summaries (A); trim noisy tool output before it enters context (D). |

### Scenario 2
| # | Ans | Why |
|---|-----|-----|
| 2.1 | C | User-scoped `~/.claude/commands/` stays personal and outside version control. |
| 2.2 | B | `context: fork` isolates verbose skill exploration in a sub-context; only the result returns. |
| 2.3 | D | `allowed-tools` is a runtime-enforced allowlist — a guarantee, not a request like prose instructions. |
| 2.4 | A | Project-level `CLAUDE.md` is what every clone receives; `/memory` verifies what actually loaded. |
| 2.5 | C | `.claude/rules/` splits a monolith into focused, path-scoped files loaded only when relevant. |
| 2.6 | B | `--resume <name>` restores that specific session's full context. |
| 2.7 | D | `fork_session` branches from a shared baseline into independent, non-contaminating explorations. |
| 2.8 | A | Stale tool results make raw resumption unreliable; reseed a fresh session with curated valid conclusions. |
| 2.9 | C | `/compact` compresses accumulated context in-session without ending it. |
| 2.10 | B, E | A scratchpad preserves exact facts; subagent delegation keeps verbose exploration out of the main context. |

### Scenario 3
| # | Ans | Why |
|---|-----|-----|
| 3.1 | D | `Task` is the only spawning mechanism — missing from `allowedTools` means no delegation is possible regardless of prompt wording. |
| 3.2 | B | Subagents don't inherit coordinator context; findings must be explicitly included in the delegation prompt. |
| 3.3 | A | Emitting multiple `Task` calls in one response spawns subagents concurrently. |
| 3.4 | C | Goal/criteria-based prompts let subagents adapt approach instead of breaking on rigid scripts. |
| 3.5 | B | Iterative refinement: evaluate → targeted re-delegation → re-synthesize until coverage criteria are met. |
| 3.6 | D | Narrow, role-scoped toolsets reduce misselection versus large shared catalogs. |
| 3.7 | A | MCP resources expose catalogs for discovery without burning exploratory tool calls. |
| 3.8 | C | Structured claim-source mappings preserved through every hop keep attribution intact into synthesis. |
| 3.9 | A, D | Surface conflicts explicitly with attribution (A); dates prevent mistaking timing differences for contradictions (D). |
| 3.10 | B, E | Layered resilience: fix transient issues locally, escalate what's unresolved (B); distinguish failure from valid-empty (E). |

### Scenario 4
| # | Ans | Why |
|---|-----|-----|
| 4.1 | A, C | Glob matches file paths; Grep searches file contents — each tool does its own job. |
| 4.2 | D | Read + Write replaces the whole file when Edit's unique-anchor requirement can't be met. |
| 4.3 | B | Incremental Grep-then-Read tracing beats broad upfront Reads that exhaust context. |
| 4.4 | A | Shared config via `.mcp.json` + env var expansion avoids committing secrets; personal server stays user-scoped. |
| 4.5 | C | Direct execution for well-scoped, single-path work; plan mode for large-scale, multi-approach work. |
| 4.6 | D | `argument-hint` prompts for missing required parameters on bare invocation. |
| 4.7 | B | `CLAUDE.md` for always-relevant standards; skills for occasional, on-demand workflows. |
| 4.8 | A | Adaptive, discovery-driven decomposition fits genuinely open-ended, high-uncertainty tasks. |
| 4.9 | C | Prompt chaining for fixed/predictable workflows; dynamic decomposition where findings drive the next step. |
| 4.10 | B, D | Each subagent config is self-contained (B); subagents share no memory across invocations (D). |

### Scenario 5
| # | Ans | Why |
|---|-----|-----|
| 5.1 | B | `-p` makes it non-interactive; `--output-format json` + `--json-schema` guarantees machine-parseable structured findings instead of scraped prose. |
| 5.2 | D | `CLAUDE.md` supplies persistent project context (standards, fixtures) to every invocation, including CI's `-p` runs — missing knowledge isn't fixed by volume or temperature. |
| 5.3 | A | Feed the prior review's findings back into context and instruct the model to report only deltas — new or still-unaddressed issues — since Claude has no memory of the last CI run otherwise. |
| 5.4 | C | A session that generated the code carries the reasoning that justified it, biasing self-review; run review in an independent instance with no generator context. |
| 5.5 | D | Vague conservatism doesn't transfer; explicit categorical criteria with concrete boundaries give the model an operable rule. |
| 5.6 | B | A high-noise category damages trust across all categories; disable and fix it offline rather than let it erode credibility everywhere. |
| 5.7 | A | Concrete examples per severity level anchor an operational definition, preventing the same issue from drifting between "critical" and "minor." |
| 5.8 | C | Few-shot examples with reasoning transfer nuanced judgment (accepted idiom vs. genuine bug) that prose alone failed to convey, and generalize to novel cases. |
| 5.9 | A, D | Batch fits latency-tolerant, non-blocking workloads (A); blocking workflows must stay synchronous since batch has no latency SLA and can take up to 24 hours (D). |
| 5.10 | B, E | Few-shot works best with a small number of targeted, reasoning-annotated examples (B) and examples that lock in exact output format for consistency (E) — not raw volume. |
